platform, and really amplify

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.980
and leverage some very
limited developer resources

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.130
to try and achieve some
really amazing results.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.380
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK so, this
is an open source project

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.980
built on your 20%
time at Google,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:57.920
our Friday afternoons where we
can work on fun things related

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:58.730
to the field.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.270
Now tie this to KubeCon, we
here at KubeCon in Copenhagen,

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.650
and you guys just gave a talk?

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:04.566
JOSH HOKE: Absolutely.

00:03:04.566 --> 00:03:08.600
So, we just gave a talk, because
the whole way that we trained,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.192
the whole way we got
data, was with Kubernetes.

00:03:11.192 --> 00:03:12.650
We had this problem
where we needed

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.640
to create hundreds, thousands,
actually millions of games

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.570
in order to get the
sort of strong model.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:21.320
So what did we do?

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.360
We used Kubernetes.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.775
We used machine learning.

00:03:24.775 --> 00:03:25.180
ANDREW JACKSON: So what
did we do with her?

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.300
JOSH HOKE: Machine
learning, that's right.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:26.970
ANDREW JACKSON:
Machine learning.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:27.860
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK, so--

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:28.800
ANDREW JACKSON: It's
kind of a punch line,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.260
but I think the
important part here

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.710
is that this is
reinforcement learning, where

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:36.750
the algorithm has to create
its own data to train on.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:42.170
And that creation of data
is a really expensive part

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.620
of the overall process.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.510
So, in other reinforcement
learning applications

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.240
like robotics, running
these simulations

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.130
that produce the next
iteration of data

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:54.440
is really computationally
expensive,

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:59.390
whereas for the Go domain-
for the domain of playing Go-

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:02.210
that can be very
easily parrallelized

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:03.755
in a very natural way.

00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:05.630
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So the
parallelization- now,

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:10.880
you guys ran a huge model to
train your implementation-

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.860
and you used Kubernetes
to orchestrate

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.570
the playing of the games.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.630
Is that right, more or less?

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:17.630
JOSH HOKE: That's right.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.279
So we use Kubernetes-
we actually use GKE-

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.547
and we created a
giant, giant cluster.

00:04:24.547 --> 00:04:25.130
And not only--

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.300
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Define giant.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.583
JOSH HOKE: I think
we were running, what

00:04:27.583 --> 00:04:28.750
do you say, like 2000 nodes?

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:29.874
ANDREW JACKSON: 2,000 GPUs.

00:04:29.874 --> 00:04:31.070
JOSH HOKE: Right so, yeah--

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.120
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: GPU instances?

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:32.828
JOSH HOKE: Right.

00:04:32.828 --> 00:04:35.180
So not only were we using
a whole bunch of machines,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.184
we were using a whole bunch
of GPUs to train our models.

00:04:39.184 --> 00:04:40.100
ANDREW JACKSON: Right.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.475
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And this problem

00:04:41.475 --> 00:04:45.164
is particularly well suited
for CUDA and GPUs, right?

00:04:45.164 --> 00:04:46.080
JOSH HOKE: Absolutely.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.210
And so, the reason why
it's suited to GPUs

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.630
is because we're running
things through machine learning

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.820
models, through neural networks.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:59.300
It's just math- just matrices-
and the thing that GPUs

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.520
are really good at is
actually running things

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.320
through matrices.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.310
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
OK, so each game

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.220
that was simulated-
some 20,000 games that

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.980
were simulated- each of
those ran in a Kubernetes pod

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.110
as a container?

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.110
JOSH HOKE: That's right.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.720
And we scheduled out
all these pods, all

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.480
with the communities
batch API, the job API,

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.370
and we set the
parallelism way up,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.290
and Kubernetes just
took care of scheduling

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:23.750
on all these machines.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:24.549
It was fantastic.

00:05:24.549 --> 00:05:26.590
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now can
I docker pull this image

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.430
and, I don't know, run a
game simulation for fun

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.065
on my laptop, obviously
not GPU accelerated?

00:05:32.065 --> 00:05:33.190
ANDREW JACKSON: Absolutely.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:34.950
If you have the GPU
accelerated version

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.341
you can do that as well.

00:05:36.341 --> 00:05:38.340
That will be a different
image, but for sure, we

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:39.120
have these available.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.286
You can pull down the image.

00:05:40.286 --> 00:05:42.190
We actually have a UI
attached to that image,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:43.940
so you can actually
watch it play, and see

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.207
it think as you do that.

00:05:46.207 --> 00:05:47.790
That's actually our
demo that we have.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.373
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: I'd
love to see that.

00:05:49.373 --> 00:05:52.802
So how long does playing
a game take a simulation?

00:05:52.802 --> 00:05:54.510
ANDREW JACKSON: Just
a couple of minutes.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:55.884
JOSH HOKE: Just
a couple minutes.

00:05:55.884 --> 00:05:58.950
If you don't have a GPU, it
can take quite a long time.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.150
ANDREW JACKSON: But it depends
how much you want it to think.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:00.610
JOSH HOKE: That's right.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:00.990
ANDREW JACKSON: That's
the real question.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.480
You want it to think a
while, it could be done.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.097
You want it to
think a long time,

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:06.180
it would take a long time.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.390
You want it to think very
quickly, won't take as long.

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:09.098
It's your choice.

00:06:09.098 --> 00:06:11.820
JOSH HOKE: But with GPUs, maybe
a couple of minutes for a game.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:12.630
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Couple
of minutes a game.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.280
ANDREW JACKSON:
Almost all of the load

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.650
is taken care of
by the GPU, there's

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.200
very little work that's
actually done by the processor

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.687
if you're using
GPU acceleration.

00:06:20.687 --> 00:06:22.270
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: All
right, fantastic.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.805
I would love to see your demo.

00:06:23.805 --> 00:06:24.930
ANDREW JACKSON: You got it.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.060
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Talk us through this.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.090
ANDREW JACKSON: All right,
so what we're seeing here

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.580
is we are seeing- well, what
we'll see in a few seconds,

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.050
I guess I should wait.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:35.580
What we're seeing here is we're
going to see Minigo think.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.357
It's going to be
trying to figure out

00:06:37.357 --> 00:06:38.440
what moves will be played.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.114
So on the left side, we see
the principal variation,

00:06:41.114 --> 00:06:43.280
and it just decided that
it wants to play this move.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.530
So now it's going to be my
turn, I'm gonna play there,

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.488
and then we're going to
see Minigo think again.

00:06:47.488 --> 00:06:49.625
So I decided to play there,
and Minigo is thinking,

00:06:49.625 --> 00:06:51.500
white will play one then
black will play two,

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:52.740
then white will play three.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:53.880
And as those
numbers move around,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.254
it's changing its
mind about what

00:06:55.254 --> 00:06:58.174
it thinks is the best
outcome for both sides.

00:06:58.174 --> 00:06:59.590
The other graphs
that we see here,

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.200
so this board up
here at the top is

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.641
the actual inferences that
are happening on the model

00:07:03.641 --> 00:07:04.140
right now.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:08.520
So each of those is 1/20 of the
total number of possible board

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.020
positions that it's looking at.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.640
So just like how a human
would play the game when

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.530
we try to think ahead
and anticipate what's

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.650
going to happen, and then, by
visualizing what will happen,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.930
we then try and
decide whether or not

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.510
we like where we're going.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.200
Minigo is doing
exactly the same thing.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:25.830
It's trying to read ahead
what the best choices are

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.830
for either person,
and essentially

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.459
figure out whether it likes
where the game is developing.

00:07:31.459 --> 00:07:33.000
So that's what the
top one is, that's

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.140
each of those inferences.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.973
The middle one is all
of the different moves

00:07:35.973 --> 00:07:37.050
that it's exploring.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.911
The bottom one is the change in
score for the given play-out.

00:07:41.911 --> 00:07:44.160
This graph that you see in
the upper right-hand corner

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.566
is how it thinks the game
is going for each side.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:47.940
As it goes up, it
thinks one side

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.356
is winning, as
it's going down, it

00:07:49.356 --> 00:07:50.880
thinks the other
side is winning.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.137
And so, all of that de-bug
information is all there.

00:07:54.137 --> 00:07:56.220
JOSH HOKE: Yet I should
say, one of the key things

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.040
is, of course Minigo things
much faster than we do.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.825
And that's why we're
only sampling 1/20.

00:08:00.825 --> 00:08:02.283
ANDREW JACKSON:
We're only sampling

00:08:02.283 --> 00:08:03.490
1/20 of its table positions.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:04.560
JOSH HOKE: Its
positions, that's right.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.700
ANDREW JACKSON:
So, as Go players,

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:06.750
this is really
interesting for us

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.420
to watch and see what it's
thinking of when it sees things

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.427
that we maybe don't
think of, or moves

00:08:11.427 --> 00:08:12.510
that we wouldn't think of.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.820
And we can also see what it
thinks the continuation is,

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:16.401
what will happen after that.

00:08:16.401 --> 00:08:18.900
So this is a really great study
tool, and it's useful for us

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:19.820
as Go players.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:20.280
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And I should say,

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.500
one thing that's
super fascinating

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.160
is, I look at this
game and I'm like,

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:24.940
this is something
that I would play.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:27.650
This actually looks like
a very human game to me.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:28.650
JOSH HOKE: That's right.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.441
This is a smaller model,
we should mention.

00:08:30.441 --> 00:08:32.010
This is a nine-by-nine
game of Go.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.016
The real game of Go is actually
four times as big as this,

00:08:35.016 --> 00:08:37.140
but this is a smaller
version that is easier for us

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:40.049
to run locally, and essentially
give a demo on a laptop

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:42.809
instead of on a big
beefy GPU machine.

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:45.907
So this is a smaller version,
it's a smaller model as well.

00:08:45.907 --> 00:08:47.740
But as a result, that
doesn't really matter.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.240
We've got full sized
models as well,

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.480
you can download those
also off of our bucket

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:52.360
with all of our data.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.650
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Are you
ready to take on a human?

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:56.670
ANDREW JACKSON: Yeah,
we've taken on humans.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.795
This has been playing in
public against anybody who

00:08:58.795 --> 00:09:00.390
wants to play it on Go servers.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.032
It's still in
development, and we're

00:09:03.032 --> 00:09:04.740
hoping that we can
replicate the results,

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.906
and then also start to
explore what else can be done

00:09:06.906 --> 00:09:09.390
with it, what can be added,
what other sorts of options

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:09.890
there are.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.015
JOSH HOKE: But there's
definitely a lot to improve.

00:09:12.015 --> 00:09:13.190
ANDREW JACKSON: A lot to do.

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:14.370
JOSH HOKE: There's always
bugs to figure out,

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:15.600
we're always making it better.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:16.830
ANDREW JACKSON: If
you want to find us,

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.871
this is all on GitHub,
right under the TensorFlow

00:09:18.871 --> 00:09:19.410
repository.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.830
So github.com/tensorflow/minigo,
we'll take any sort of pull

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.020
requests, comments,
issues, complaints,

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.760
conspiracy theories,
whole thing.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.760
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: What a
fascinating, fascinating

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:30.750
project.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:34.110
Just tying this back to
Kubernetes one last bit.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:37.590
So, Kubernetes provided you
some infrastructure underneath.

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:39.370
Without a Kubernetes,

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.810
What were your options, I guess?

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.270
ANDREW JACKSON: So,
after I had finished

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:46.830
the initial
implementation of this,

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.260
I realized that the hard part
wasn't actually implementing

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:50.051
the neural network.

00:09:50.051 --> 00:09:51.990
The hard part was
figuring out how

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:56.100
to do this huge amount of
self-play and create the data.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.680
It's not just 20,000 games,
it's 20,000 games per model,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.180
and you need to do
500 models, maybe.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:03.729
So it's a huge amount
of this compute

00:10:03.729 --> 00:10:05.270
that I needed to
figure out how was I

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:08.017
going to do that when each
game takes minutes to play.

00:10:08.017 --> 00:10:10.100
And luckily I knew a guy
who worked on Kubernetes,

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.780
and I was able to say, hey
Josh, how should I do this?

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.289
Should I like, script a bunch
of bash to manage VMs directly?

00:10:17.289 --> 00:10:18.080
JOSH HOKE: Totally.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:18.980
Bash is the right option.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:20.570
ANDREW JACKSON: Oh man,
I had all of these other,

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.460
like, how am I going
to get this at scale

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.020
with only the open source tools?

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.270
Because I didn't have any
of the Google infrastructure

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:27.670
to lean on.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:28.700
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: I guess
that's the other piece, right,

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.491
the infrastructure.

00:10:29.491 --> 00:10:31.400
You didn't have to
provision a single VM,

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:32.690
you didn't have to find a GPU.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:34.606
ANDREW JACKSON: I did
have to do a lot of work

00:10:34.606 --> 00:10:36.980
in getting the code to
work inside a docker image,

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:40.655
because this involved
making those docker

00:10:40.655 --> 00:10:41.780
images is very performant.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.800
Like, Docker's
abstraction is, well,

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.520
don't worry about what the
actual silicon is underneath.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:48.980
But TensorFlow and
GPUs were very much

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:53.000
trying to compile with all of
the processor optimizations,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.670
we're trying to compile with
the latest NVIDIA drivers.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:57.980
Getting all of that to work
inside the docker image

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:01.290
was non-trivial, but it's
also gotten a lot easier.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.320
I think in particular
with the launch of GPUs

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:07.850
on GKE, that whole situation
with getting the latest

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:10.390
drivers, getting
CUDA DNN installed,

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:12.360
that's all come a long way.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.420
And even when we started
this, it was only,

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.820
I guess six months ago.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.839
It's come a long
way, just since then.

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:19.880
So it's pretty exciting--

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:20.240
JOSH HOKE: Super exciting.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.114
ANDREW JACKSON: To see
that sort of progress.

00:11:22.114 --> 00:11:24.260
But that hard part was
getting that performance

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.260
inside those containers.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.681
So it is interesting to
see that the containers are

00:11:27.681 --> 00:11:29.930
no barrier to this sort of
high performance computing.

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:32.536
If you want to use GPUs
and go as fast as you can,

00:11:32.536 --> 00:11:34.410
you want to use all of
the extended processor

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.260
instructions, you can do
that too in your instances

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.385
with is a little bit of
care in how you craft them.

00:11:38.385 --> 00:11:40.150
So I think that's a
pretty interesting.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:40.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
That's fantastic.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.950
Josh, Andrew, thank
you for your time.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:43.950
JOSH HOKE: Thank you.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.033
ANDREW JACKSON: Thank you.

00:11:45.033 --> 00:11:46.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

